  "image_name": "02a5bbc46f224e1365a1dc46fac74437.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how neurons are shaped by both cell-intrinsic and cell-extrinsic mechanisms. The central neuron integrates signals from surrounding glial cells, the extracellular matrix (ECM), synaptic inputs, metabolism, and its own activity to control gene expression and ultimately synapse and firing properties.\n\nA major theme is bidirectional communication between neurons and glia. Astrocytes, microglia, and oligodendrocytes influence synaptogenesis, refinement, receptor recycling, ECM remodeling, metabolic support, and conduction speed through myelination. In turn, the neuron produces growth factors, ECM components, receptors, and ion channels that affect nearby cells and its own connectivity. Synaptic input and activity appear to trigger signaling pathways that regulate growth factor production, synapse formation and strengthening, and action potential firing.\n\nInside the neuron, mitochondria and metabolism feed into chromatin regulation and epigenetics, with metabolites acting on chromatin remodelers. These nuclear mechanisms regulate expression of activity-regulated genes and other programs that alter synaptic properties and firing patterns. Overall, the image presents the neuron as a hub where external cues and internal metabolic and epigenetic states converge to shape development, plasticity, and circuit function.",
